\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The sign edge meets the Rayleigh hypothesis}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{The sign edge of \ref{fdip-012H} has matrix
##{\begin{pmatrix}8&2\\2&2\end{pmatrix}}
in the basis of its two group elements. Both double cosets have
size #{1}, so dividing by the square root of the size leaves
the matrix unchanged, and the displayed matrix equals its
transpose. Its characteristic polynomial is #{t^2-10t+12}.
Let the ordered field contain a positive square root of #{52},
and set #{\lambda=\frac{10+\sqrt{52}}{2}}. Then #{\lambda} is an
eigenvalue, #{\langle v,Av\rangle\le\lambda\langle v,v\rangle}
for every #{v}, and equality holds exactly when
#{Av=\lambda v}. The off-diagonal entries are positive, so
\ref{fdip-011X} applies in this field.}

\proof{
\p{The discriminant of #{t^2-10t+12} is #{100-48=52}. Thus
#{\lambda} and #{\frac{10-\sqrt{52}}{2}} are the two roots.
Their sum is #{10} and their product is #{12}, so both are
positive, and #{\lambda} is the larger one. Then
#{{{\lambda}-8}=\frac{\sqrt{52}-6}{2}} and
#{{{\lambda}-2}=\frac{\sqrt{52}+6}{2}}. Hence
##{({\lambda}-8)({\lambda}-2)
 =\frac{(\sqrt{52}-6)(\sqrt{52}+6)}{4}
 =\frac{52-36}{4}=4,}
so #{{{\lambda}-8}=\frac{4}{{\lambda}-2}}. For #{v=(x,y)},
##{\lambda{\langle v,v\rangle}-{\langle v,Av\rangle}
 =\frac{4}{{\lambda}-2}x^2-4xy+({\lambda}-2)y^2
 =\frac{1}{{\lambda}-2}\bigl(2x-({\lambda}-2)y\bigr)^2.}
The right side is nonnegative. It vanishes exactly when
#{2x=({\lambda}-2)y}. The vector
#{\bigl({\lambda}-2,\,2\bigr)} is nonzero because
#{{{\lambda}-2}} is positive. Its image under the matrix has
second coordinate #{2({\lambda}-2)+4=2\lambda}. The first
coordinate is #{8({\lambda}-2)+4=8{\lambda}-12}. The eigenvalue
equation #{\lambda^2-10\lambda+12=0} rearranges to
#{\lambda({\lambda}-2)=8{\lambda}-12}, so the vector is an
eigenvector for #{\lambda}. The other root is different, so
that line is the whole eigenspace. Equality in the quadratic
form therefore holds exactly when #{Av=\lambda v}. The support
graph joins the two indices. Since the matrix itself is
entrywise positive, \ref{fdip-011X} gives a simple largest
eigenvalue and a strictly smaller absolute value for the other
eigenvalue.}
}
